				Example(caption: "The four ways it says no", captionPt: "As quatro formas de dizer não", code: "$ hive export taps.hive\nhive: `hive export` needs a target, and the one it knows is `--target android/arm64`.\n\n$ hive export taps.hive --target android/amd64\nhive: `--target android/amd64` needs a C cross-compiler, which is the one thing a machine with the Go toolchain on it cannot be assumed to have. `android/arm64` is the only Android target Go links on its own, and it is what a handset is.\n\n$ hive export taps.hive --target linux/arm64\nhive: `hive export` knows one format, and it is Android's. It takes `--target android/arm64`; `linux/arm64` has no app to be packaged into.\n\n$ hive export server.hive --target android/arm64\nserver.hive:1: `server.hive` opens no window, so there is nothing for an app to show and no way to start it. `hive build` is what compiles a program that serves or prints.", codePt: "$ hive export taps.hive\nhive: `hive export` needs a target, and the one it knows is `--target android/arm64`.\n\n$ hive export taps.hive --target android/amd64\nhive: `--target android/amd64` needs a C cross-compiler, which is the one thing a machine with the Go toolchain on it cannot be assumed to have. `android/arm64` is the only Android target Go links on its own, and it is what a handset is.\n\n$ hive export taps.hive --target linux/arm64\nhive: `hive export` knows one format, and it is Android's. It takes `--target android/arm64`; `linux/arm64` has no app to be packaged into.\n\n$ hive export server.hive --target android/arm64\nserver.hive:1: `server.hive` opens no window, so there is nothing for an app to show and no way to start it. `hive build` is what compiles a program that serves or prints."),
