		Slide(
			title: "Containers",
			titlePt: "Contêineres",
			download: false,
			shot: "",
			explanation: "`hive container main.hive` writes a Dockerfile for the program into the folder the command was run in, which is also the build's context. It checks the program first, as `hive check` would, so a program that does not compile gets its errors rather than an image build that fails ten minutes later.\n\nNothing has to be installed to build the image but Docker:\n\n- **The first stage downloads Go, and the compiler that wrote the file**, for the platform being built for: amd64 on an ordinary machine and arm64 on an Apple-silicon one, so the build is native either way. The compiler is pinned in `ARG HIVEC_VERSION`, so an image never follows a newer release on its own, and `docker build --build-arg HIVEC_VERSION=v9.9.9 .` builds with another one.\n- **The second stage is the executable alone**, on `distroless/static`: no Go, no compiler, not even a shell — only the certificates an HTTPS call needs and the time zones.\n\nWhat is not a template is read off the program. A `hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...)` becomes an `EXPOSE 8080`, with a comment saying where the number came from, and a program that serves nothing exposes nothing. An import that names a repository puts `git` in the build stage, and a program that opens a database runs `go mod tidy` before anything compiles. Everything beside the Dockerfile goes into the build, so a `.dockerignore` is what narrows it.\n\nWhat comes out is an ordinary Dockerfile, and editing it is expected. A `Dockerfile` already in the folder is never written over: ours is called `Dockerfile-hive-container` instead, the command says which it wrote, and building from it takes `docker build -f Dockerfile-hive-container .`. This tour is served out of an image built this way.",
			explanationPt: "`hive container main.hive` escreve um Dockerfile para o programa na pasta em que o comando foi rodado, que é também o contexto do build. Ele checa o programa antes, como o `hive check` faria, então um programa que não compila recebe os erros dele em vez de um build de imagem que falha dez minutos depois.\n\nNada precisa estar instalado para construir a imagem além do Docker:\n\n- **O primeiro estágio baixa o Go, e o compilador que escreveu o arquivo**, para a plataforma para a qual se está construindo: amd64 numa máquina comum e arm64 numa de silício Apple, então o build é nativo de qualquer jeito. O compilador fica fixado em `ARG HIVEC_VERSION`, então uma imagem nunca segue um lançamento mais novo sozinha, e `docker build --build-arg HIVEC_VERSION=v9.9.9 .` constrói com outro.\n- **O segundo estágio é só o executável**, sobre `distroless/static`: sem Go, sem compilador, nem sequer um shell — só os certificados de que uma chamada HTTPS precisa e os fusos horários.\n\nO que não é template é lido do programa. Um `hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...)` vira um `EXPOSE 8080`, com um comentário dizendo de onde o número veio, e um programa que não serve nada não expõe nada. Um import que nomeia um repositório coloca `git` no estágio de build, e um programa que abre um banco de dados roda `go mod tidy` antes de qualquer coisa compilar. Tudo que está ao lado do Dockerfile entra no build, então um `.dockerignore` é o que o estreita.\n\nO que sai é um Dockerfile comum, e editá-lo é esperado. Um `Dockerfile` que já esteja na pasta nunca é sobrescrito: o nosso se chama `Dockerfile-hive-container` em vez disso, o comando diz qual escreveu, e construir a partir dele pede `docker build -f Dockerfile-hive-container .`. Este tour é servido a partir de uma imagem construída assim.",
			examples: [
				Example(caption: "Writing it, and running what it wrote", captionPt: "Escrevendo, e rodando o que foi escrito", code: `$ hive container main.hive
Wrote Dockerfile for main.hive (0s)
$ docker build -t main .
$ docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main

# A Dockerfile already there is left alone, and ours is named for itself.
$ hive container main.hive
Wrote Dockerfile-hive-container for main.hive (0s)
$ docker build -f Dockerfile-hive-container -t main .`, codePt: `$ hive container main.hive
Wrote Dockerfile for main.hive (0s)
$ docker build -t main .
$ docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main

# Um Dockerfile que já esteja lá é deixado em paz, e o nosso leva nome próprio.
$ hive container main.hive
Wrote Dockerfile-hive-container for main.hive (0s)
$ docker build -f Dockerfile-hive-container -t main .`),
				Example(caption: "What it writes", captionPt: "O que ele escreve", code: `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1
#
# Builds and runs main.hive, from nothing but this folder.
#
#   docker build -t main .
#   docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main

##############################################################################
# Stage 1 — download Go and the compiler, then build
##############################################################################
FROM debian:bookworm-slim AS builder

# What Docker is building for: amd64 on an ordinary machine, arm64 on an
# Apple-silicon one or a Graviton.
ARG TARGETARCH

# ... curl, then Go itself for that architecture ...

# The compiler itself — this one, rather than whatever release is newest on
# the day the image is built.
ARG HIVEC_VERSION=v0.2.10
RUN curl -fsSL \
      "https://github.com/R0DR160HM/hive-lang/releases/download/$\{HIVEC_VERSION}/hivec-linux-$\{TARGETARCH}" \
      -o /usr/local/bin/hivec \
 && chmod +x /usr/local/bin/hivec

# A static binary, so the runtime stage below needs no libc.
ENV CGO_ENABLED=0

WORKDIR /app
COPY . .

RUN hivec build /app/main.hive \
 && test -x /app/main

##############################################################################
# Stage 2 — runtime
##############################################################################
FROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12 AS runtime
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /app/main /usr/local/bin/main

# 8080 is where this program serves: hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...) says so.
EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/main"]`, codePt: `# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1
#
# Builds and runs main.hive, from nothing but this folder.
#
#   docker build -t main .
#   docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 main

##############################################################################
# Stage 1 — download Go and the compiler, then build
##############################################################################
FROM debian:bookworm-slim AS builder

# What Docker is building for: amd64 on an ordinary machine, arm64 on an
# Apple-silicon one or a Graviton.
ARG TARGETARCH

# ... curl, then Go itself for that architecture ...

# The compiler itself — this one, rather than whatever release is newest on
# the day the image is built.
ARG HIVEC_VERSION=v0.2.10
RUN curl -fsSL \
      "https://github.com/R0DR160HM/hive-lang/releases/download/$\{HIVEC_VERSION}/hivec-linux-$\{TARGETARCH}" \
      -o /usr/local/bin/hivec \
 && chmod +x /usr/local/bin/hivec

# A static binary, so the runtime stage below needs no libc.
ENV CGO_ENABLED=0

WORKDIR /app
COPY . .

RUN hivec build /app/main.hive \
 && test -x /app/main

##############################################################################
# Stage 2 — runtime
##############################################################################
FROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12 AS runtime
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=builder /app/main /usr/local/bin/main

# 8080 is where this program serves: hive.net.httpServe(8080, ...) says so.
EXPOSE 8080

ENTRYPOINT ["/usr/local/bin/main"]`),
			],
		),
		Slide(
			title: "Where to go next",
			titlePt: "Para onde ir agora",
			download: true,
			shot: "",
			explanation: "That is the tour. Everything in it runs on the compiler that served it to you. The download link below is the latest release — one executable, built for Linux, macOS and Windows on `amd64` and `arm64`. Put it on your `PATH` as `hive`, with Go 1.24 or newer beside it, and every example here is one file away from running for real.\n\nThe repository is the reference this tour is not:\n\n- `spec/` is the language written down properly, one numbered document per part — the grammar, the type system, mutability, the bounds pass, the builtins, the standard library, the lowering to Go. It is where to look once the tour has told you a thing is true and you want to know exactly how true.\n- `examples/` holds whole programs rather than fragments, numbered and each one runnable, and `./examples/run` runs every one of them against its expected output, which is what keeps them honest.\n- `src/` is the compiler, written in Hive and built by itself, so it is also the largest Hive program there is to read.",
			explanationPt: "Este é o tour. Tudo nele roda no compilador que o serviu a você. O link de download abaixo é o último lançamento — um único executável, construído para Linux, macOS e Windows em `amd64` e `arm64`. Coloque-o no seu `PATH` como `hive`, com o Go 1.24 ou mais novo ao lado, e todo exemplo daqui está a um arquivo de rodar de verdade.\n\nO repositório é a referência que este tour não é:\n\n- `spec/` é a linguagem escrita direito, um documento numerado por parte — a gramática, o sistema de tipos, a mutabilidade, a passagem de limites, as funções nativas, a biblioteca padrão, a tradução para Go. É onde procurar quando o tour lhe disse que algo é verdade e você quer saber exatamente o quanto.\n- `examples/` guarda programas inteiros em vez de fragmentos, numerados e cada um executável, e `./examples/run` roda cada um deles contra a saída esperada, que é o que os mantém honestos.\n- `src/` é o compilador, escrito em Hive e construído por ele mesmo, então é também o maior programa Hive que existe para ler.",
			examples: [
				Example(caption: "One file, every command", captionPt: "Um arquivo, todos os comandos", code: `func greeting(): Str \{
	return "it works"
}

proc main(): void \{
